Message-ID: <awNSOxZbs1s.zovsY9fpT-cJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <awNSOxZbs1s.CXGBrBk1m8IJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <awNSOxZbs1s.yfCSfNf7nEgJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <awNSOxZbs1s.CXGBrBk1m8IJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/awNSOxZbs1s/m/zovsY9fpT-cJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

If you have a set of parameters or generated quantities,
you can declare a generated quantity and define it as
the max of the set.  Then you'll get the posterior.

For instance, if you want the max normal from a set of
K normals (i.e., an order statistic), use:

   parameters {
     real y;
   }
   model {
     y ~ normal(0,1);
   }
   generated quantities {
     real y_max;
     y_max <- max(y);
   }

You can get all the ranks using sorting.

- Bob

