> I think Bob is saying we can have the same inits by setting init=0, so it makes sense to try that first.  Otherwise, if all you have are the Stan 2.0.1 runs, you have a couple options.  One option is to take them as is.  I don't think the inferences are _too_ horrible; in the bivaraiate normal case, we were getting a sd of 0.96 instead of 1.0, and according to Mike B., the bivariate case happens to be the worst case of that bug.  Another option would be to take the draws from the Stan 2.0.1 runs, consider them as a correct summary of the posterior distribution, and do some posterior predictive checks.  But really I wouldn't feel so comfortable until it's running in 2.1--

 > I agree with Bob that it sounds like there could be a problem with
 > the model (Bob is invoking the Folk Theorem here, I believe).

Maybe Andrew's folk theorem has broader applicability, but I
just meant that the main reason we've seen this kind of failure in
the past is models that have declared constraints that don't match
the support of the model.

- Bob

