On 1/15/14, 10:55 PM, Jarrett Barber wrote:
> I have a simulator (of tree growth) written in C++ whose returned value I'd like
 > to use as the mean of a Gaussian specification in Stan.  The simulator has
 > about 30 parameters plus various other inputs.  All parameters spaces are subsets
 > of the reals.  I'm hoping that I can simply modify mymodel.cpp, generated by Stan,
 > in the (hopefully) obvious place(s), then link/compile as usual, letting Stan do
 > the rest, e.g., gradients, etc.

I think Daniel's writing some general instructions for people who want to do this.

You have to:

1.  Make sure you declare the parameters with appropriate constraints.  Stan assumes
the support of the log probability function matches the declared constraints on
parameters.  Then you leave the parameter transforms in place.

2.  In general, you have to code a templated version of your function, with different
template parameters for each argument.  You can get by leaving the data or transformed
data variables as doubles.  You can test independently of the model by making sure
you can instantiate the arguments that will be parameters, transformed parameters, or
local variables with stan::agrad::var.  This will also let you test the gradients, and
it's a good idea to test vs. a finite-difference implementation.

Then you have to call our built-in functions rather than the built-in C++ library functions
to make sure auto-diff will work.  But the compiler will let you know where you need to
do that.

If you send us the signature of your function, I can show you what the signature needs
to look like.

An alternative is to add your new function to Stan directly.  This is cleaner,
but we don't yet have a way to make it easy to extend, so you have to modify our
source and compile with that.  There are some sketchy instructions on how to do this
here:

https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/wiki/Contributing-New-Functions-to-Stan

- Bob

From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Wed Jan 15 22:39:11 2014
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Wed, 15 Jan 2014 22:39:11 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Can't get the sesame street code to work
Message-ID: <DjnZocbQbXo.aIwoHld_0RUJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <DjnZocbQbXo.IMYRkUeWAMkJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <DjnZocbQbXo.IMYRkUeWAMkJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/DjnZocbQbXo/m/aIwoHld_0RUJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

On 1/14/14, 2:27 PM, Christopher Grainger wrote:
 > I'm using the following from the Stan example code. There seems to be a
 > problem with the data model. I keep getting the
 > following error and the model does not get past the warmup stage:
 > Informational Message: The current Metropolis proposal is about to be rejected becuase of
 > the following issue: Invalid
 > value of Sigma_yt: Error in function validate transformed params N4stan5agrad3varE: Sigma_ytSigma_yt is not positive
 > definite. Sigma_yt(0,0) is 2.4018295391036435e-13:0.
 >
 > If anyone has any advice, please let me know.

Thanks for the error report.  Two parts to the response.

1.  The underlying problem is that we mistranslated the
original BUGS model, which has lines like

   d ~ normal(0, .001);

BUGS uses a precision-based parameterization of normal, whereas
Stan uses a standard-deviation-based parameterization.  So these
should be normal(0,31.6) to match the BUGS code.

There are other issues with that translation, too.  It's
not using vectorization (not that it'll matter much here)
and I believe all of the transformed parameters should just
be local variables in the model (otherwise you're going to
get a bunch of junk in the output).  I made both of those changes, too.

I only fixed sesame_street1.stan, which now runs with reasonable
mixing.  It takes a while to warmup, but then samples very quickly
once it's adapted.

I've bcc-ed the original author of these and am going to
ask that they be reviewed for this kind of error and that they're
checked that they get the right answer.

Sorry about this --- we should be more careful in this kind
of thing as users will assume we've done them right since they're
our models to begin with!

2. The messages you're getting are just warnings.  They weren't
the source of the problem.

What's happening is that the gradient-based approximation
of the posterior is only approximate and following the gradient
can take you outside of support on highly-constrained parameters
like covariance matrices.

You'll still see a few of them with the fixed model.

- Bob

P.S.  Here's the text of the revised model:

data {
   int<lower=0> J;
   int<lower=0> N;
   int<lower=1,upper=J> siteset[N];

   vector[2] yt[N];
   vector[N] z;
}
parameters {
   vector[2] ag[J];

   real b;
   real d;
   real<lower=-1,upper=1> rho_ag;
   real<lower=-1,upper=1> rho_yt;
   vector[2] mu_ag;
   real<lower=0,upper=100> sigma_a;
   real<lower=0,upper=100> sigma_g;
   real<lower=0,upper=100> sigma_t;
   real<lower=0,upper=100> sigma_y;
}

model {

   vector[J] a;
   vector[J] g;

   matrix[2,2] Sigma_ag;
   matrix[2,2] Sigma_yt;
   vector[2] yt_hat[N];

   //data level
   Sigma_yt[1,1] <- pow(sigma_y,2);
   Sigma_yt[2,2] <- pow(sigma_t,2);
   Sigma_yt[1,2] <- rho_yt*sigma_y*sigma_t;
   Sigma_yt[2,1] <- Sigma_yt[1,2];

   // group level
   Sigma_ag[1,1] <- pow(sigma_a,2);
   Sigma_ag[2,2] <- pow(sigma_g,2);
   Sigma_ag[1,2] <- rho_ag*sigma_a*sigma_g;
   Sigma_ag[2,1] <- Sigma_ag[1,2];

   for (j in 1:J) {
     a[j] <- ag[j,1];
     g[j] <- ag[j,2];
   }

   for (i in 1:N) {
     yt_hat[i,2] <- g[siteset[i]] + d * z[i];
     yt_hat[i,1] <- a[siteset[i]] + b * yt[i,2];
   }

   //data level
   sigma_y ~ uniform (0, 100);
   sigma_t ~ uniform (0, 100);
   rho_yt ~ uniform(-1, 1);

   d ~ normal (0, 31.6);
   b ~ normal (0, 31.6);

   //group level
   sigma_a ~ uniform (0, 100);
   sigma_g ~ uniform (0, 100);
   rho_ag ~ uniform(-1, 1);

   mu_ag ~ normal (0, 31.6);

   for (j in 1:J)

     ag[j] ~ multi_normal(mu_ag,Sigma_ag);

   //data model
   for (i in 1:N)

     yt[i] ~ multi_normal(yt_hat[i],Sigma_yt);

}

>
> data {
>    int<lower=0> J;
>    int<lower=0> N;
>    int<lower=1,upper=J> siteset[N];
>    matrix[N,2] yt;
>    vector[N] z;
> }
> parameters {
>    matrix[J,2] ag;
>    real b;
>    real d;
>    real<lower=-1,upper=1> rho_ag;
>    real<lower=-1,upper=1> rho_yt;
>    vector[2] mu_ag;
>    real<lower=0,upper=100> sigma_a;
>    real<lower=0,upper=100> sigma_g;
>    real<lower=0,upper=100> sigma_t;
>    real<lower=0,upper=100> sigma_y;
> }
> transformed parameters {
>    vector[J] a;
>    vector[J] g;
>    cov_matrix[2] Sigma_ag;
>    cov_matrix[2] Sigma_yt;
>    matrix[N,2] yt_hat;
