>
> I used student t marginals and a normal copula as a good example above of why copulas are helpful. Basically, if you
> were to instead fit a multivariate student t distribution, then you effectively assume the degrees of freedom parameter
> is the same across every variable. The student t + normal copula would often be fit by first fitting each of the
> variables with a student t distribution with each having its own degrees of freedom. Then you would create a new
> multivariate variable applying the appropriate student t inverse pdf to each variable. This puts each variable between 0
> and 1. To fit the normal copula to this, I might apply a standard normal cdf to that and estimate the resulting
> correlation matrix.
>

