> > > >      >
> > > >      >          y_sim ~ normal(mu,sigma);
> > > >      >
> > > >      >     in the model, then you do joint inference for (mu,sigma,y_sim).
> > > >      >     In this case, I think the answers should be equivalent other than
> > > >      >     that generated_quantities will be more efficient --- it shouldn't change
> > > >      >     your inferences for mu and sigma.
> > > >      >
> > > >      >     You can't do anything with the posterior means directly in
> > > >      >     Stan, because you can't express it as a log probability function.
> > > >      >     You could, though, generate a fit, take the posterior means,
> > > >      >     then plug that back in somewhere else.
> > > >      >
> > > >      >     Maybe if you wrote down a sketch of the BUGS model for what you're
> > > >      >     trying to do, it would be easier for us to understand exactly
> > > >      >     what you're trying to do.
> > > >      >
> > > >      >     - Bob
> > > >      >
> > > >      >     On 1/11/14, 12:27 AM, John Hall wrote:
> > > >      >      > That's precisely what I'm doing. I'm generating fake data and fitting a model and checking to see if it
> > > >      >     reconstructs the
> > > >      >      > parameters...What I'm saying is that I'm getting some output from Stan (using the fake data) that's a
> > > >      >     distribution for
> > > >      >      > some parameters and can I use that distribution (and others) in Stan in another separate Stan
> > > >     estimation/fit (again
> > > >      >      > where the data was fake). In a Gibbs sampling approach, I can just add extra steps to each iteration that
> > > >     use the
> > > >      >     fake data.
> > > >      >      >
> > > >      >      > More detail that may not be all that necessary:
> > > >      >      > I'm testing fitting some finance asset pricing models with Stan, particularly for use in portfolio
> > > >     management.
> > > >      >     There's a
> > > >      >      > few papers out there that fit traditional asset pricing models with a hierarchical Bayesian approach, but
> > > >     they
> > > >      >     focus on
> > > >      >      > the means and variances. The important thing for portfolio management is not just the means and variances,
> > > >     but
> > > >      >     also the
> > > >      >      > correlations between different securities. Factor models are a common approach to dealing with the large
> > > >     number of
> > > >      >      > correlations to be estimated.
> > > >      >      >
> > > >      >      > If I fit a hierarchical model of the form:
> > > >      >      >
> > > >      >      > y ~ N(a[s], sigma^2) where s is an indicator for industry and sigma is an idiosyncratic variance
> > > >      >      >
> > > >      >      > then it will ignore the correlations. Alternately, a common factor model like:
> > > >      >      >
> > > >      >      > y ~ N(a[s] + b * x_mkt, sigma^2) where x_mkt is the return on the market
> > > >      >      >
> > > >      >      > is essentially using the aggregate to predict the underlying, which you have criticized on your blog. I can
> > > >      >     reformulate
> > > >      >      > this in terms of a structured multivariate normal distribution, but it doesn't easily scale for more
> > > >     sophisticated
> > > >      >      > distributions that are common in finance.
> > > >      >      >
> > > >      >      > So my first step is to fit a cross-sectional factor model or fundamental factor model (it's from an old
> > > >     paper by
> > > >      >     Barr
> > > >      >      > Rosenberg), which is a regression cross-sectionally in each period of time using the parameters of some
> > > >     factors.
> > > >      >     You do
> > > >      >      > that each period in time and then the coefficients to that regression would be like the returns to different
> > > >      >     factors.
> > > >      >      > You can then forecast the returns and use the factor return forecasts to generate forecasts for the
> > > >     stocks. So for
> > > >      >      > instance, I might regress the stock returns for a period against the price earnings ratio for each stock
> > > >     and an
> > > >      >      > indicator for each industry. The coefficients then represent the returns to a P/E factor and industry
> > > >     factors.
> > > >      >      >
> > > >      >      > I've already done the above in Stan (excluding the P/E variable) using a hierarchical approach. The result is
> > > >      >     that each
> > > >      >      > period there is a simulated distribution for the industry/factor return for that period.
> > > >      >      >
> > > >      >      > Suppose I take the mean of the distribution of returns in each period. This is like treating factor
> > > >     returns like
> > > >      >     they
> > > >      >      > are known, which means I can apply any type of analysis I want in Stan.The approach I'm using is like
> > > >     regressing
> > > >      >      >
> > > >      >      > y ~ N(a[s] + b * x[s], sigma^2) where x[s] is the time series of industry returns
> > > >      >      >
> > > >      >      > so that if you simulate from the posterior predictive of the x's, then the y's will be properly
> > > >     correlated. It's
> > > >      >     also
> > > >      >      > bottom-up, avoiding the issue mentioned above. It's flexible in that I can separately fit the x's to
> > > >     incorporate
> > > >      >     things
> > > >      >      > like time-varying means, variances, and fat tails only on the factors.
> > > >      >      >
> > > >      >      > However, by assuming those are just returns instead of a distribution, I'm losing the information about the
> > > >      >     estimation
> > > >      >      > error. I can incorporate them just fine in a Gibbs Sampling approach since I have the simulated returns in
> > > >     each
> > > >      >      > iteration and can just add extra steps to perform any additional analysis. I'm just not sure if I can do
> > > >     it in Stan.
> > > >      >      >
> > > >      >      > On Friday, January 10, 2014 4:21:22 PM UTC-5, Andrew Gelman wrote:
> > > >      >      >
> > > >      >      >     Hi, I don't quite understand the question, but I think the way to go is to just fully specify the
> > > >      >     hierarchical model
> > > >      >      >     rather than jumping in the middle to direct the simulation.  But if you have an idea and you want to
> > > >     try it out,
> > > >      >      >     what i suggest is our old friend, fake-data simulation.  Start by assuming every part of the model, then
> > > >      >     simulate
> > > >      >      >     fake data, then fit your model to the fake data and check that you're doing a reasonable job
> > > >     reconstructing the
> > > >      >      >     parameters.
> > > >      >      >     Good luck!
> > > >      >      >     A
> > > >      >      >
> > > >      >      >     On Jan 10, 2014, at 9:52 PM, John Hall wrote:
> > > >      >      >
> > > >      >      >>     I've generated some sample panel data with multivariate normal returns for 10 groups of 10 random
> > > >     variables
> > > >      >     each,
> > > >      >      >>     such that there is correlation between groups but no correlation within groups (the only thing that is
> > > >      >     observed is
