Hi, my suggestion is not so much for speed as for stability.  With some data, the gamma prior can lead to unstable results.  It can also be a very strong prior, dominating the data.  We discuss in the 2006 paper and also in BDA.

On Jan 27, 2014, at 7:49 PM, John Hall wrote:

> Just to clarify with respect to the variances/standard deviations, you just recommend removing the inv_gamma statements from the model statement without making any changes to the parameters. I didn't notice a speed up doing that, but I'll take your word for it that I should do it. And I'll read your 2006 paper to try to figure out why.
>
> I can put some more information on the mean, but for a simple test I just felt like putting something there that might work on any type of data. In a more complicated version I would include the prior in the data step to allow for better customization.
>
> On Monday, January 27, 2014 1:06:07 PM UTC-5, Andrew Gelman wrote:
> > As Bob would say, Andrew doesn't like those inv_gamma(0.001, 0.001) priors.  Really, you have no reason to touch those variances at all.  The sd's are just fine to work with on their own.  Andrew also doesn't like normal(0,100), as he thinks it's better to put in some weak prior information to control the scale of a problem.
> >
> > On Jan 27, 2014, at 5:41 PM, John Hall wrote:
> >
> > > I think I've stumbled across what I needed, albeit it's a little slow. A simple set of code is below (using just one index). This approach can be used for hierarchical models when you want to be able to model some kind of systematic factor and exposures to such a factor. My example was meant for an asset pricing model (in finance) that estimates a bottom-up market factor instead of using the observed market returns.
> > >
> > > I generated some x's that are effectively the cross-sectional mean in each period of the y's. I then regress each of the y's against x. Importantly, I do this in two separate steps to ensure the x's will be tied down.
> > >
> > > When I was originally trying to get this to work in one Stan file, I had tried this format, but crucially without the first y statement (which I can seem to only be able to do in the loop). This led to there being too many parameters. While the x's in that method may have been correlated with the original data used to generate the data, they had significantly different means (perhaps even different signs) and variances. This led to the beta_x's and mu_y's being way off what is sensible, even if they would produce the correct posterior predictive distributions. Including the first y statement ties everything down.
> > >
> > > data {
> > >   int<lower=0> N;
> > >   int<lower=0> T;
> > >   vector[T] y[N];
> > > }
> > >
> > > parameters {
> > >   real mu_x;
> > >   real<lower=0> var_x;
> > >   vector[T] x;
> > >   real beta_x[N];
> > >   real mu_y;
> > >   real<lower=0> var_y;
> > > }
> > > transformed parameters {
> > >   real<lower=0> std_x;
> > >   real<lower=0> std_y;
> > >
> > >   std_x <- sqrt(var_x);
> > >   std_y <- sqrt(var_y);
> > > }
> > > model {
> > >   mu_x ~ normal(0, 100);
> > >   var_x ~ inv_gamma(0.001, 0.001);
> > >   x ~ normal(mu_x, std_x);
> > >   beta_x ~ normal(0, 100);
> > >   var_y ~ inv_gamma(0.001, 0.001);
> > >   mu_y ~ normal(0, 100);
> > >
> > >   for (n in 1:N) {
> > >     y[n] ~ normal(x, std_y);
> > >     y[n] ~ normal(mu_y + beta_x[n] * x, std_y);
> > >   }
> > > }
> > >
> > >
> > > On Tuesday, January 14, 2014 5:07:02 AM UTC-5, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> > > > On 1/14/14, 3:02 AM, John Hall wrote:
> > > > > For the Gibbs thing, I was just summarizing how I write the program to do the Gibbs sampling more than anything. I
> > > > > typically would write seperate functions for estimating the distribution for a parameter and simulating from that
> > > > > distribution. It's the same thing as what you're talking about though, just as separate steps.
> > > >
> > > > You can do that in Stan using the generated quantities block.
> > > > The parameters are declared as parameters, the model is in
> > > > the model, then the quantities to generate conditional on
> > > > the parameters are declared and defined in the generated quantities
> > > > block.
> > > >
> > > > This is one of the reasons we built the generated quantities --- to
> > > > generate predictive quantities based on the parameters.
> > > >
> > > > If you do Gibbs, you have to have the joint probability anyway,
> > > > assuming your model's consistent --- the conditionals should
> > > > be conditionals of a joint probability.  Technically, all you
> > > > need is something proportional to the posterior p(parameters|data),
> > > > which itself is proportional to the joint p(parameters,alpha) by
> > > > Bayes's rule.  Sorry if this is all obvious.
> > > >
> > > > - Bob
> > > >
> > > > > On Monday, January 13, 2014 7:15:01 PM UTC-5, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> > > > >
> > > > >     Yes, Stan requires you to write down the full probability
> > > > >     function as does any kind of full Bayesian modeling.
> > > > >
> > > > >     Your algorithm sketch doesn't look like a Gibbs sampler to me.
> > > > >     If you have N parameters theta[1],...,theta[N], and full probability
> > > > >     function p(theta[1],...,theta[N]),  then Gibbs initializes theta, then
> > > > >     does
> > > > >
> > > > >     for (i in 1:I)
> > > > >         for (n in 1:N)
> > > > >           sample theta[n] from p(theta[n] | theta[1],..,theta[n-1],theta[n+1],...,theta[N])
> > > > >
> > > > >     I don't understand your estimate/simulate notation.
> > > > >
> > > > >     The short answer is that you can't mix simulation and sampling
> > > > >     in Stan.  You can sample and do simulations based on the samples,
> > > > >     but you can't then feed the simulations back into the sampler in any way.
> > > > >
> > > > >     - Bob
> > > > >
> > > > >
> > > > >
> > > > >
> > > > >
> > > > >     On 1/14/14, 12:39 AM, John Hall wrote:
> > > > >      > Thanks for the reply Bob. I hadn't used BUGS before using Stan and had just coded my own Gibbs samplers, so I
> > > > >     thought
> > > > >      > this would be easier to attach what I've been working on (I have it set up so the un-rar'ed folder is in C). I also
> > > > >      > sketched a Gibbs sampler of what I'm trying to do below, more or less. The S_test_stan.r file is the main file.
> > > > >     It calls
> > > > >      > the S_gen_data.r file to create the data and then fits some Stan models.
> > > > >      >
> > > > >      > The key line is 60:
> > > > >      > y_sim_index_cutdown <- colMeans(aperm(y_sim_index, c(2, 1, 3)))
> > > > >      > In the previous section, I fit the Stan model cross-sectionally as I described above. The y_sim_index variable is
> > > > >     the
> > > > >      > array containing all these results. Thus, this line is like taking the posterior mean in each period (which is
> > > > >     what you
> > > > >      > seem to recommend). I then use these means in subsequent analysis, treating them as though they are observed data.
> > > > >      >
> > > > >      > In a Gibbs sampler, I could just use the y_sim_index posterior samples directly in the next stage of the
> > > > >     analysis. For
> > > > >      > instance, the following sequence I give a headline of "Fit Simple Hierarchical Model with Index as Regressor" in the
> > > > >      > code regresses the y_bottom variable against the respective index (calculated by that line 60 above). In a Gibbs
> > > > >      > sampler, in an iteration I can use the y_sim_index in the next line (see sketch). It seems like the impression I'm
> > > > >      > getting is that Stan requires the formula for the distribution. I'm wondering if that's the case or if there's
> > > > >     any way
> > > > >      > to use simulated variables at all the way I would with Gibbs sampling.
> > > > >      >
> > > > >      > I'm essentially throwing away all the rest of the information about the uncertainty in these means. I considered
> > > > >     that I
> > > > >      > could calculate the covariance in each time period and assume that the measurement error is multivariate normal. I'm
> > > > >      > worried that this might be slow (I can't even get the multivariate normal-inverse Wishart distribution to work
> > > > >      > properly), but it seems possible. For this type of data, multivariate normal seems like a good assumption, but it
> > > > >     may
> > > > >      > not always make sense.
> > > > >      >
> > > > >      > Gibbs sketch:
> > > > >      >
> > > > >      > for i in 1:(n_burn+n_gibbs) {
> > > > >      >    #cross-sectional estimation
> > > > >      >    for j in 1:n_j {
> > > > >      >      for t in 1:T {
> > > > >      >        estimate x[t, j], the mean in each time period t for group j of variable y
> > > > >      >        simulate x[t, j]
> > > > >      >      }
> > > > >      >    }
> > > > >      >    # time-series estimation
> > > > >      >    for n in 1:N {
> > > > >      >      for equation: y[n] ~ N(beta_y[n] * x[index[n]], sigma[n]^0.5)
> > > > >      >      estimate beta_y[n]
> > > > >      >      simulate beta_y[n]
> > > > >      >      estimate sigma[n]
> > > > >      >      simulate sigma[n]
> > > > >      >      Note the x's are all from the cross-sectional step above. They change with each Gibbs iteration.
> > > > >      >    }
> > > > >      >
> > > > >      >    if i>n_burn {
> > > > >      >      save relevant variables
> > > > >      >    }
> > > > >      > }
> > > > >      >
> > > > >      >
> > > > >      > On Saturday, January 11, 2014 3:28:17 PM UTC-5, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> > > > >      >
> > > > >      >     I'm also a little unclear on what the problem is.
> > > > >      >
> > > > >      >     Are you asking whether we can do cut like in BUGS?
> > > > >      >     The closest we can come is using the generated quantities
> > > > >      >     block to generate new variables based on fitted values.
> > > > >      >     It uses the parameters in each sample, so propagates that
> > > > >      >     uncertainty.
> > > > >      >
> > > > >      >     For example,
> > > > >      >
> > > > >      >         data {
> > > > >      >           int N;
> > > > >      >           real y[N];
> > > > >      >           int N_sim;
> > > > >      >         }
> > > > >      >         parameters {
> > > > >      >           real mu;
> > > > >      >           real<lower=0> sigma;
> > > > >      >         model {
> > > > >      >           y ~ normal(mu,sigma);
> > > > >      >         }
> > > > >      >         generated quantities {
> > > > >      >           real y_sim[N_sim];
> > > > >      >           for (n in 1:N_sim)
> > > > >      >             y_sim[n] ~ normal_rng(mu,sigma);
> > > > >      >         }
> > > > >      >
> > > > >      >     This fits the parameters mu and sigma based on the data
> > > > >      >     y, then generates M new variates y_sim.  There's no feedback
> > > > >      >     from the generated quantities to the model.  If you instead
> > > > >      >     declared y_sim as a parameter and included
> > > > >      >
> > > > >      >          y_sim ~ normal(mu,sigma);
> > > > >      >
> > > > >      >     in the model, then you do joint inference for (mu,sigma,y_sim).
> > > > >      >     In this case, I think the answers should be equivalent other than
> > > > >      >     that generated_quantities will be more efficient --- it shouldn't change
> > > > >      >     your inferences for mu and sigma.
> > > > >      >
> > > > >      >     You can't do anything with the posterior means directly in
> > > > >      >     Stan, because you can't express it as a log probability function.
> > > > >      >     You could, though, generate a fit, take the posterior means,
> > > > >      >     then plug that back in somewhere else.
> > > > >      >
> > > > >      >     Maybe if you wrote down a sketch of the BUGS model for what you're
> > > > >      >     trying to do, it would be easier for us to understand exactly
> > > > >      >     what you're trying to do.
> > > > >      >
> > > > >      >     - Bob
> > > > >      >
> > > > >      >     On 1/11/14, 12:27 AM, John Hall wrote:
> > > > >      >      > That's precisely what I'm doing. I'm generating fake data and fitting a model and checking to see if it
> > > > >      >     reconstructs the
> > > > >      >      > parameters...What I'm saying is that I'm getting some output from Stan (using the fake data) that's a
> > > > >      >     distribution for
> > > > >      >      > some parameters and can I use that distribution (and others) in Stan in another separate Stan
> > > > >     estimation/fit (again
> > > > >      >      > where the data was fake). In a Gibbs sampling approach, I can just add extra steps to each iteration that
> > > > >     use the
> > > > >      >     fake data.
> > > > >      >      >
> > > > >      >      > More detail that may not be all that necessary:
> > > > >      >      > I'm testing fitting some finance asset pricing models with Stan, particularly for use in portfolio
> > > > >     management.
> > > > >      >     There's a
> > > > >      >      > few papers out there that fit traditional asset pricing models with a hierarchical Bayesian approach, but
> > > > >     they
> > > > >      >     focus on
> > > > >      >      > the means and variances. The important thing for portfolio management is not just the means and variances,
> > > > >     but
> > > > >      >     also the
> > > > >      >      > correlations between different securities. Factor models are a common approach to dealing with the large
> > > > >     number of
> > > > >      >      > correlations to be estimated.
