> >      >      >      > Rosenberg), which is a regression cross-sectionally in each period of time using the parameters of
> >     some
> >      >     factors.
> >      >      >     You do
> >      >      >      > that each period in time and then the coefficients to that regression would be like the returns to
> >     different
> >      >      >     factors.
> >      >      >      > You can then forecast the returns and use the factor return forecasts to generate forecasts for the
> >      >     stocks. So for
> >      >      >      > instance, I might regress the stock returns for a period against the price earnings ratio for each
> >     stock
> >      >     and an
> >      >      >      > indicator for each industry. The coefficients then represent the returns to a P/E factor and industry
> >      >     factors.
> >      >      >      >
> >      >      >      > I've already done the above in Stan (excluding the P/E variable) using a hierarchical approach. The
> >     result is
> >      >      >     that each
> >      >      >      > period there is a simulated distribution for the industry/factor return for that period.
> >      >      >      >
> >      >      >      > Suppose I take the mean of the distribution of returns in each period. This is like treating factor
> >      >     returns like
> >      >      >     they
> >      >      >      > are known, which means I can apply any type of analysis I want in Stan.The approach I'm using is like
> >      >     regressing
> >      >      >      >
> >      >      >      > y ~ N(a[s] + b * x[s], sigma^2) where x[s] is the time series of industry returns
> >      >      >      >
> >      >      >      > so that if you simulate from the posterior predictive of the x's, then the y's will be properly
> >      >     correlated. It's
> >      >      >     also
> >      >      >      > bottom-up, avoiding the issue mentioned above. It's flexible in that I can separately fit the x's to
> >      >     incorporate
> >      >      >     things
> >      >      >      > like time-varying means, variances, and fat tails only on the factors.
> >      >      >      >
> >      >      >      > However, by assuming those are just returns instead of a distribution, I'm losing the information
> >     about the
> >      >      >     estimation
> >      >      >      > error. I can incorporate them just fine in a Gibbs Sampling approach since I have the simulated
> >     returns in
> >      >     each
> >      >      >      > iteration and can just add extra steps to perform any additional analysis. I'm just not sure if I
> >     can do
