Hmm. This could be a sticking point. The problem is that the induced
prior is obtained by sampling and hence is not one of the usual
densities. How about generating the density and then reading it back in
as a vector in a second analysis, then randomly sample from that vector?
Does that make sense?

>
>> Then follows logarithmic pooling of the two priors to produce a
>> single prior for each output, and then
>> usual Bayesian inference can be conducted. I'm not sure how to do
>> this in Stan, but I am willing to try. If anyone has
