Message-ID: <UExogz5Hv5M.ROWOYEzQ4ygJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <UExogz5Hv5M.lpt6-cWHElkJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <UExogz5Hv5M.lpt6-cWHElkJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/UExogz5Hv5M/m/ROWOYEzQ4ygJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Once you're extracting using permuted=TRUE, you should not sample at random or thin by taking every 10th or whatever, you should just take the first few in order from the permuted sample.  Why?  Because the permutation order gets saved, so next time you want to grab them, you'll again get the first 30 or however many draws from this permuted set.

On Jan 30, 2014, at 11:10 AM, Tamas Papp wrote:

> Hi,
>
> If I want, say, 30 random draws from the merged postrior sample in
> Rstan, what's the idiomatic way to do it?
>
> If the result of extract(fit, permuted=TRUE) was a data.frame, I could
> just use sample() and []. But since it is a list of arrays, with
> generally unknown rank, I can't do that. Is there an idiomatic way that
> I missed? I know how to write a function for it, just being curious.
>
> Best,
>
> Tamas
